<h1>JavaScript Applications</h1>

<p>
JavaScript is one of the most popular programming languages. It is used in many areas such as web development, mobile apps, desktop applications, server-side development, IoT, AI, and game development.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>1. Web Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is the primary programming language for <b>client-side web development</b>.
It is used with <b>HTML</b> and <b>CSS</b> to create dynamic, interactive, and user-friendly web pages.
</p>

<p>
JavaScript can:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Handle user events (click, hover, keyboard input, etc.).</li>
    <li>Validate forms.</li>
    <li>Create animations.</li>
    <li>Update page content without reloading the page.</li>
    <li>Improve the overall user experience.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>2. Single Page Application (SPA) Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript libraries and frameworks such as <b>React</b>, <b>Angular</b>, and <b>Vue.js</b> are used to build <b>Single Page Applications (SPA)</b>.
</p>

<p>
In a Single Page Application, only the required content is updated without refreshing the entire web page.
</p>

<p>
Advantages of SPA:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Fast loading</li>
    <li>Smooth user experience</li>
    <li>Less page reload</li>
    <li>Better performance</li>
</ul>

<p>
SPAs are commonly used in:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Admin Dashboards</li>
    <li>E-commerce Websites</li>
    <li>Social Media Platforms</li>
    <li>Online Banking Applications</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>3. Server-Side Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is also used for <b>backend development</b> with <b>Node.js</b>.
</p>

<p>
Node.js allows developers to build complete <b>Full Stack Applications</b> using a single programming language for both frontend and backend.
</p>

<p>
JavaScript is commonly used for:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>REST APIs</li>
    <li>Authentication Systems</li>
    <li>Real-Time Applications</li>
    <li>Chat Applications</li>
    <li>Online Collaboration Tools</li>
</ul>

<p>
Examples:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>WhatsApp Web</li>
    <li>Google Meet</li>
    <li>Zoom</li>
    <li>Discord</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Quick Revision</h2>

<ul>
    <li>JavaScript is mainly used for Web Development.</li>
    <li>Works with HTML and CSS.</li>
    <li>Creates Dynamic and Interactive Web Pages.</li>
    <li>React, Angular and Vue.js are used to build SPAs.</li>
    <li>Node.js is used for Backend Development.</li>
    <li>JavaScript can be used for Full Stack Development.</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h2>4. Mobile Application Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript can also be used to develop <b>mobile applications</b>.
Using frameworks like <b>React Native</b> and <b>Ionic</b>, developers can
build cross-platform mobile apps.
</p>

<p>
These frameworks allow developers to write code once and deploy it on
both <b>Android</b> and <b>iOS</b> devices.
</p>

<p>
Advantages:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Single Codebase</li>
    <li>Cross-Platform Development</li>
    <li>Faster Development</li>
    <li>Easy Maintenance</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>5. Desktop Application Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript can also be used to build <b>desktop applications</b> using
frameworks like <b>Electron.js</b>.
</p>

<p>
Electron allows developers to create cross-platform desktop applications
using HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
</p>

<p>
Popular desktop applications built with Electron include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Visual Studio Code</li>
    <li>Discord</li>
    <li>Slack</li>
    <li>Postman</li>
</ul>

<p>
Advantages:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Cross-Platform Support</li>
    <li>Lightweight Applications</li>
    <li>Easy Development using Web Technologies</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>6. Progressive Web Apps (PWA)</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is widely used to build <b>Progressive Web Apps (PWA)</b>.
A PWA provides an app-like experience while running inside a web browser.
</p>

<p>
Features of PWA:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Fast Loading</li>
    <li>Offline Support</li>
    <li>Push Notifications</li>
    <li>Responsive Design</li>
    <li>Installable on Mobile and Desktop</li>
</ul>

<p>
PWAs work even with slow or limited internet connections.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>7. Internet of Things (IoT)</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is also used in <b>Internet of Things (IoT)</b> development.
Its event-driven and asynchronous nature makes it suitable for controlling
smart devices and sensors.
</p>

<p>
Libraries like <b>Johnny-Five</b> help developers interact with IoT hardware.
</p>

<p>
JavaScript can be used to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Control Sensors</li>
    <li>Read Device Data</li>
    <li>Automate Smart Devices</li>
    <li>Communicate with Hardware</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Quick Revision</h2>

<ul>
    <li>React Native and Ionic → Mobile Apps</li>
    <li>Electron.js → Desktop Applications</li>
    <li>PWA → Web App with App-like Experience</li>
    <li>PWA supports Offline Mode and Push Notifications.</li>
    <li>Johnny-Five → IoT Development</li>
    <li>JavaScript can control Smart Devices and Sensors.</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h2>8. Data Visualization</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is widely used for <b>Data Visualization</b>. It helps developers
display data in the form of charts, graphs and interactive dashboards.
</p>

<p>
Popular JavaScript libraries for Data Visualization include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Chart.js</li>
    <li>D3.js</li>
    <li>Plotly.js</li>
</ul>

<p>
These libraries make it easy to create attractive and interactive
visualizations directly in the browser.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>9. Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML)</h2>

<p>
Although Python is the most popular language for AI and Machine Learning,
JavaScript can also be used in this field.
</p>

<p>
Libraries like <b>TensorFlow.js</b> allow developers to build, train and
run Machine Learning models directly in the browser or with Node.js.
</p>

<p>
Applications include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Image Recognition</li>
    <li>Object Detection</li>
    <li>Face Detection</li>
    <li>Text Classification</li>
    <li>AI-based Web Applications</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>10. Game Development</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is also used for <b>Game Development</b>.
It allows developers to create 2D and 3D browser-based games.
</p>

<p>
Popular JavaScript game engines include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Phaser.js</li>
    <li>Babylon.js</li>
    <li>Three.js</li>
</ul>

<p>
These libraries help developers create interactive and high-performance
games for the web.
</p>

<hr>

<h1>ECMAScript (ES)</h1>

<p>
<b>ECMAScript (ES)</b> is the official standard specification for
JavaScript.
</p>

<p>
It defines the syntax, features and behavior that JavaScript engines
must follow.
</p>

<p>
ECMAScript is developed and maintained by <b>ECMA International</b>.
</p>

<p>
Other scripting languages like <b>JScript</b> and
<b>ActionScript</b> also follow the ECMAScript standard.
</p>

<p>
The main purpose of ECMAScript is to ensure consistent JavaScript
behavior across different browsers and environments.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Benefits of ECMAScript</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Standardized JavaScript Syntax</li>
    <li>Cross-Browser Compatibility</li>
    <li>Modern Language Features</li>
    <li>Better Performance</li>
    <li>Easy Maintenance</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Quick Revision</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Chart.js, D3.js and Plotly.js → Data Visualization</li>
    <li>TensorFlow.js → AI & Machine Learning</li>
    <li>Phaser.js, Babylon.js and Three.js → Game Development</li>
    <li>ECMAScript (ES) → Official JavaScript Standard</li>
    <li>Maintained by ECMA International</li>
    <li>Provides standard syntax and browser compatibility</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h1>JavaScript Engines</h1>

<p>
A <b>JavaScript Engine</b> is a program that executes JavaScript code.
It converts JavaScript code into machine code so that the computer can understand and run it.
</p>

<p>
JavaScript engines are used in web browsers, web servers and other JavaScript environments.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Popular JavaScript Engines</h2>

<h3>1. V8 Engine</h3>

<p>
The <b>V8 Engine</b> was developed by <b>Google</b> for
<b>Google Chrome</b>.
</p>

<p>
It is also used in Chromium-based browsers such as:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Microsoft Edge</li>
    <li>Brave</li>
    <li>Opera</li>
</ul>

<p>
V8 is also the execution engine used by <b>Node.js</b>.
It is known for its high performance and fast execution.
</p>

<hr>

<h3>2. JavaScriptCore (JSC)</h3>

<p>
<b>JavaScriptCore (JSC)</b> was developed by <b>Apple</b>.
</p>

<p>
It is used in:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Safari Browser</li>
    <li>All iOS Browsers</li>
</ul>

<p>
JavaScriptCore is optimized for speed and smooth performance.
</p>

<hr>

<h3>3. SpiderMonkey</h3>

<p>
<b>SpiderMonkey</b> was developed by <b>Mozilla</b>.
</p>

<p>
It is the JavaScript engine used in the <b>Mozilla Firefox</b> browser.
</p>

<hr>

<h3>4. Chakra / ChakraCore</h3>

<p>
<b>Chakra</b> was developed by <b>Microsoft</b>.
</p>

<p>
It was mainly used in the older versions of <b>Microsoft Edge</b>.
</p>

<p>
Later, Microsoft switched to the Chromium-based Edge browser, which now uses the <b>V8 Engine</b>.
</p>

<hr>

<h1>Ways to Add JavaScript in HTML</h1>

<h2>1. Inline JavaScript</h2>

<p>
JavaScript code is written directly inside an HTML element.
</p>

<pre>
&lt;button onclick="alert('Hello World!')"&gt;
    Click Here
&lt;/button&gt;
</pre>

<hr>

<h2>2. Internal JavaScript</h2>

<p>
JavaScript code is written inside the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag in the same HTML file.
</p>

<pre>
&lt;script&gt;
console.log("Hello World");
console.warn("Warning Message");
console.error("Error Message");
document.write("Hello JavaScript");
&lt;/script&gt;
</pre>

<hr>

<h2>3. External JavaScript</h2>

<p>
JavaScript code is written in a separate <b>.js</b> file and linked with HTML.
</p>

<pre>
&lt;script src="script.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</pre>

<p>
Advantages of External JavaScript:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Reusable Code</li>
    <li>Easy Maintenance</li>
    <li>Cleaner HTML File</li>
    <li>Better Performance</li>
</ul>

<hr>

